[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"End-to-End Testing in React Native: Detox vs. Appium"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"End-to-end (E2E) testing ensures that your React Native application works seamlessly from the user's perspective across real devices and emulators. Two of the most popular tools for this task are Detox and Appium. This tutorial explores their architectures, use cases, and how to set them up."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Tools"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Detox is a grey-box testing and automation framework specifically built for mobile apps. It runs tests on the device while synchronizing with the app's native event loop, which drastically reduces flakiness. Appium, on the other hand, is a black-box cross-platform automation tool supporting multiple languages and platforms, interacting with the app via WebDriver protocols."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up Detox"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To get started with Detox, you need to install the command-line tools and configuration dependencies. Run the following command in your terminal to install applesimutils and the detox CLI globally:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"brew tap wix/brew && brew install applesimutils
npm install -g detox-cli"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, install Detox as a devDependency in your React Native project and initialize the configuration:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"npm install detox --save-dev
npm init detox -r jest"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing a Detox Test"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a test file inside your e2e folder. Below is an example of a simple login test using Detox and Jest:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"describe('Example', () => {
  beforeEach(async () => {
    await device.reloadReactNative();
  });

  it('should show welcome screen', async () => {
    await expect(element(by.id('welcome'))).toBeVisible();
  });
});"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up Appium"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Appium relies on Node.js and WebDriver. Install the Appium server and the XCUITest driver for iOS or UiAutomator2 for Android:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"npm install -g appium
appium driver install uiautomator2
appium driver install xcuitest"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing an Appium Test"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Using WebDriverIO with Appium allows you to write clean JavaScript or TypeScript E2E tests. Here is a basic configuration snippet:"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"const opts = {
  path: '/wd/hub',
  port: 4723,
  capabilities: {
    platformName: 'Android',
    deviceName: 'Android Emulator',
    app: '/path/to/app.apk',
    automationName: 'UiAutomator2'
  }
};"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences and Best Practices"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Detox provides superior synchronization, making tests inherently faster and less flaky for React Native apps."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Appium excels when testing cross-platform ecosystems that include native apps, hybrid apps, and mobile web views in a single suite."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always assign unique testID props to your React Native components to ensure reliable element selectors across both frameworks."}]
